    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so like, if i have a signal that's super messy or has a bunch of random interference, the transform just separates the useful stuff from the junk? how does it actually \"see\" those hidden frequencies if they're all tangled up in the time domain?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, the transform is essentially a blindfold test that forces the signal to align with pure sine waves. By scanning against every possible frequency, it automatically cancels out the visual clutter of the time domain to isolate those specific hidden patterns. Imagine shredding a complex essay into individual letters; you can't read the story anymore, but sorting those letters counts how many words, or in this case, how much energy exists at each specific frequency.",
      "source": "target",
