    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok i think i'm getting it. so the \"clutter\" or the \"noise\" is just frequencies we don't care about. but if the signal is really chaotic, is there a limit to how much detail we can actually extract? like can it always find the pattern no matter how messy it is?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "No, there are firm mathematical limits to what the transform can recover, mostly due to how the signal is sampled and how long the data is. If the sampling rate is too low, fundamental frequencies get lost entirely (aliasing), and if the data segment is too short, you only get a blurry, approximate picture of the true frequencies. So while it's incredibly powerful, it cannot magically create information that was never recorded in the first place.",
      "source": "target",
